<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Long-horizon agents compact their history to continue within a finite
context window, but a textual summary alone may not support every
subsequent decision. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Remory</span>, a neural memory network
that supplements the summary with a bounded sequence of soft memory
tokens. Given the history and summary, the network learns to generate
tokens that help a frozen LLM approximate the continuation it would
produce with the full history. The tokens are conditioned on the summary
and appended after it, forming an analogue of a residual connection along
the sequence dimension. On SummHay, <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Remory</span> improves source attribution
at nearly unchanged insight coverage and approaches the full-context
joint score using only 5.2% of the input positions. Across long-horizon
agent benchmarks, Qwen3.8-27B and GLM-5.3-Flash show consistent gains
with residual memory. Both models also exhibit substantially fewer
repeated tool outputs and tool errors on BrowseComp and Terminal-Bench 2.1.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Long-horizon agents work within a finite context window. As observations,
tool outputs, and decisions accumulate, they must compact their history
to continue. A common practice today is to replace earlier interaction
with a textual summary. For example, popular coding agents such as
Claude Code and Codex CLI provide <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/compact</span> to free context
space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026a</a>)</cite>.
The summary becomes a checkpoint from which the agent resumes its work.
Input length follows a sawtooth pattern: it grows with interaction,
drops at compaction, and grows again (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>a).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A textual checkpoint changes how prior context is available to the agent.
Preserving the main thread does not guarantee that the summary will
support every subsequent decision, and the relevance of earlier context
can change as the task unfolds. We do not assume that the information
needed beyond the summary can be identified or enumerated in advance.
The challenge is to sustain useful access to prior context across
compaction without keeping the entire interaction in the active window.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.11287v2/headline_benchmarks.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/158;" width="476" height="158"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>BrowseComp and Terminal-Bench 2.1 scores. Pale and solid bars
compare the same local actor without and with residual memory.
Gray bars are published frontier references, with their original
harnesses and protocols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google DeepMind, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terminal-Bench Team, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026b</a>)</cite>;
they are not controlled comparisons with our runs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.11287v2/context_and_human_memory.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/151;" width="476" height="151" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Context compaction and human memory.
(a) Input length across successive requests in an agent run, showing
repeated growth and sharp reductions.
(b) Memory systems illustrated using the Harvard–Oxford
atlas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harvard–Oxford Atlas Contributors, 2026</a>)</cite>: prefrontal regions (blue), hippocampus
(cyan), and association cortex (purple).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Human cognition illustrates how a limited active workspace can support
sustained work. While contemporary agent contexts span roughly
0.2–1.0 million token positions, human working memory holds only a few
chunks under controlled conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baddeley, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cowan, 2001</a>)</cite>.
Yet people can pursue complex projects over months or years, repeatedly
drawing on details, experience, and learned skills in long-term
memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ericsson &amp; Kintsch, 1995</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Squire &amp; Dede, 2015</a>)</cite>. This ability depends
on access to stored knowledge as well as active maintenance.
Hippocampal indexing theory describes one form of <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">neural indexing</em>:
partial cues activate an index that reinstates distributed cortical
representations of an earlier experience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Teyler &amp; Rudy, 2007</a>)</cite>.
Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>b illustrates the complementary memory
systems involved <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldman-Rakic, 1995</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumaran et al., 2016</a>)</cite>.
For agents, this suggests pairing an explicit checkpoint with a learned
neural representation that keeps prior context useful as work continues.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Remory</span>, a neural memory network for context compaction.
Given a history and its generated summary, we train the network to
generate a bounded sequence of soft memory tokens that can be appended
after the summary, helping a frozen LLM approximate the continuation
it would produce with the full history. We call this <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">residual memory</em>
because the soft tokens are both conditioned on the summary and appended
after it, forming an analogue of a residual connection along the sequence
dimension. The summary supplies a readable checkpoint, while the learned
tokens supplement it during subsequent decisions. Exact records remain
accessible through ordinary archive and file-retrieval tools.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">On SummHay, residual tokens improve source attribution at nearly unchanged
insight coverage. This supports an associative interpretation: the memory
helps connect recovered claims to supporting evidence. Across long-horizon
agent benchmarks, Qwen3.8-27B and GLM-5.3-Flash show consistent gains
with residual memory. Residual memory also reduces repeated tool outputs
and tool errors for both models on BrowseComp and Terminal-Bench 2.1.
<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Remory</span> pushes the 320B-parameter
GLM-5.3-Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Z.ai, 2026a</a>)</cite> towards frontier-level performance
(Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Residual Memory</h2>

<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.11287v2/iclr_residual_compensation.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/191;" width="476" height="191"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Residual compensation and training.
(a) Codex CLI passes the <span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/compact</span> summary to the next actor call.
(b) The <span id="S2.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Remory</span> network uses that summary to condition memory construction from the
active context; summary and continuous memory jointly support subsequent actions.
Archive access remains an ordinary tool action in either setting.
(c,d) Qwen3.8-27B teacher–student top-1 agreement during Stage I
reconstruction and Stage II residual training. The residual panel compares
reconstruction initialization with
random initialization under the same residual objective and data order.
Curves are optimization diagnostics from the training streams.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>A memory supplement to the summary</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Residual memory is constructed by conditioning on a generated summary
and appending learned soft tokens after it
(Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Residual Memory ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a,b)).
At compaction boundary <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the previous summary and residual memory, when present,
together with the interaction since the last boundary. Given the new
summary <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the memory network constructs</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}=\mathcal{C}_{\phi}(H_{i};S_{i}),\qquad|M_{i}|\leq m." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}=\mathcal{C}_{\phi}(H_{i};S_{i}),\qquad|M_{i}|\leq m.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of vectors in the frozen actor’s input space.
The concatenation <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[S_{i};M_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[S_{i};M_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> forms an analogue of a residual connection
along the sequence dimension. The actor reads persistent instructions, this
sequence, and then new interaction. The same process repeats at later compactions,
allowing memory to carry information across successive boundaries.
Archive excerpts enter through ordinary tool results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The network uses frozen actor features and learned queries to predict
memory vectors in parallel, with gated cross-attention to the summary.
Each block of up to 1,024 source positions produces 64 soft tokens.
Hierarchical compression bounds the final memory at 4,096 slots while
preserving source order. Source encoding and intermediate representations
may be larger.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For Qwen3.8-27B, we adapt the DFlash
architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>; for GLM-5.3-Flash, we use DFlash-2
with grouped dynamic convolutions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inco AI, 2026</a>)</cite>.
The resulting memory networks have 1.94B and 1.502B trainable parameters,
respectively. Their output is continuous memory rather than vocabulary
tokens. Gradients through the frozen actor train this interface without
changing the actor’s parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Learning through a frozen actor</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Residual Memory ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c,d) shows optimization behavior
during the two training stages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage I: reconstruction.</span> The teacher receives a source passage
and an instruction to repeat it. The student replaces the passage with
its soft memory and receives the same instruction. Both predict the
source text, teaching the memory network to preserve source information.
We use text fields from MMFineReason-Full-2.3M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage II: summary-conditioned continuation.</span> Starting from Stage I,
the teacher receives the original interaction history. The student receives
the corresponding fixed summary and memory constructed from that history.
Both predict the same subsequent assistant response. The memory encoder
never receives the future response. This stage uses reasoning and coding
trajectories, including prompts from
Open-PerfectBlend <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labonne, 2024</a>)</cite>, with precomputed
summaries and Qwen-generated responses. Examples follow a curriculum
ordered by preceding-context length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In both stages, the teacher and student share the same frozen actor
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a target response <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, their predictions at position <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{j}=p_{\theta}(\cdot\mid C_{\mathrm{full}},y_{&lt;j}),\qquad q_{j}=p_{\theta}(\cdot\mid C_{\mathrm{mem}},y_{&lt;j})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>full</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>mem</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}=p_{\theta}(\cdot\mid C_{\mathrm{full}},y_{&lt;j}),\qquad q_{j}=p_{\theta}(\cdot\mid C_{\mathrm{mem}},y_{&lt;j}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where both contexts include the persistent instructions. We optimize
the memory parameters through student-to-teacher reverse KL:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\frac{\sum_{j}w_{j}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q_{j}\|p_{j})}{\sum_{j}w_{j}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\frac{\sum_{j}w_{j}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q_{j}\|p_{j})}{\sum_{j}w_{j}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The loss covers the full vocabulary and complete response, with positive
weights that mildly emphasize earlier positions. Only <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is updated.
In Stage II, the summary is already available, directing memory toward
the remaining prediction gap. Memory may overlap with summary content;
it need not reconstruct every omitted fact.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Source attribution after compaction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SummHay evaluates query-relevant insight recovery and source attribution
from long document collections <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laban et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We evaluate all
92 queries from ten collections with Qwen3.8-27B under four representations:
raw context, summary alone, summary plus an additional text supplement
(summary++), and summary plus residual memory. Summary++ uses the same
additional-position budget as the memory condition. The actor, query,
decoding, and base summary are fixed across the compacted conditions.
Answer generation has no tools, archive access, or source retrieval;
gold insights and citations are used only for scoring.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>SummHay on the same 92 queries. Summary++ adds text under
the residual memory’s additional-position budget. Higher is better;
all queries, including generation and formatting failures, are retained.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Representation</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Coverage</span></th>
