<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Coverage measures recovered insights; citation F1 measures attribution;
the joint score combines both. We use GPT-5.6-Sol with the benchmark
coverage prompts. Coverage and joint score average over all 621 reference
insights, while citation F1 averages over covered insights.
Adding residual memory increases citation F1 by 4.04 points and joint
score by 3.55, with a coverage change of 0.40
(Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Source attribution after compaction ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; gains are computed before rounding).
A paired collection bootstrap gives a 95% interval of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[+0.74,+6.21]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.74</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6.21</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[+0.74,+6.21]</annotation></semantics></math>
for the joint-score gain, conditional on the generated and judged outputs.
On the 553 insights covered by both summary conditions, citation F1 still
rises from 57.31 to 61.41. The larger attribution gain at nearly unchanged
coverage suggests that memory helps connect recovered claims to evidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Mean inputs contain 96,872 positions for raw context, 1,393 for summary
alone, and 5,024 for summary plus memory. The latter uses 5.2% of the
raw-context positions and approaches its joint score, with lower coverage
and higher citation F1. The equal-budget text supplement remains close
to summary alone. In this setting, the learned supplement preserves
attribution cues more effectively than allocating the same extra
positions to generated text.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>End-to-end agent performance</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate BrowseComp research tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
Terminal-Bench 2.1 command-line tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al., 2026</a>)</cite>,
AutomationBench workflows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shepard &amp; Salimans, 2026</a>)</cite>, and JobBench
professional tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Each within-actor comparison
holds weights, the Codex CLI harness, prompts, tools, decoding, budgets,
and compaction policy fixed; both configurations can retrieve exact history.
Qwen and GLM use 262,144-position and one-million-position context windows,
respectively. The intervention adds up to 4,096 residual slots at compaction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use the full task sets: 1,266 BrowseComp and 89 Terminal-Bench tasks
for both actors (one run), and 600 AutomationBench and 65 JobBench tasks
for Qwen (five runs). Terminal-Bench and AutomationBench use task
verifiers; BrowseComp and JobBench use matched GPT-5.6-Sol judges.
Residual checkpoints complete both training stages.</p>
</div>
