<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Qwen improves by 9.8 points on AutomationBench and 7.6 on JobBench
(Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 End-to-end agent performance ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), extending the gains to business workflows
and professional-task rubric credit. It gains 4.5 points on Terminal-Bench
and 3.0 on BrowseComp; GLM gains 3.3 and 4.1, respectively
(Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.2 End-to-end agent performance ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The improvements therefore also hold for a
stronger actor with a larger context window. Published frontier scores
in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provide context; the controlled
evidence comes from the within-actor comparisons.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Repeated tool outputs fall by 17.9–29.8% and errors by 25.3–49.7%
across the four actor–benchmark pairs in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.2 End-to-end agent performance ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Repeats count identical outputs from the same tool within a task, excluding
planning and polling; errors count failed commands or explicit tool errors.
These aggregate reductions are consistent with better use of earlier
feedback, although complete trajectories differ in their actions and length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Generation cost falls in every reported comparison except JobBench, where
the higher score accompanies a rise from $3.09 to $3.21 per task.
The estimates price actor and summary tokens at September 2026 uncached provider rates,
counting residual positions as input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alibaba Cloud, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Z.ai, 2026b</a>)</cite>.
They exclude source-feature encoding, memory-network execution, and tools,
so they measure token-priced generation rather than total execution cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>The first action after compaction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We examine 488 paired next actions from 100 sampled Qwen BrowseComp
trajectories, holding the persistent prompt and summary fixed.
Following rubric-based planning evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>,
GPT-5.6-Sol compares anonymized actions against criteria fixed from
the preceding history. Candidate reasoning is withheld and counterfactual
calls remain unexecuted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Residual actions receive 345 wins (70.7%), 21 ties (4.3%), and 122 losses
(25.0%). With the checkpoint fixed, the preference links the memory
channel to the first post-compaction decision. It is a judgment of proposed
actions, not a measurement of the outcomes those counterfactual actions
would have produced.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Case studies</h3>

<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Live Trading.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Live Trading follows two concurrent Qwen paper-trading accounts under
the same context policy. At the September 23 cutoff, settled ROI is
<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-11.86\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>11.86</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-11.86\%</annotation></semantics></math> without residual and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+5.11\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5.11</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+5.11\%</annotation></semantics></math> with it; logged generation costs
are $303.81 and $170.30. ROI counts resolved markets against an initial
$20,000 bankroll. These two trajectories differ in market selection and
timing, limiting return comparisons.
Both accounts retain the growth-and-reset pattern
(Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Live Trading. ‣ 3.4 Case studies ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Remory: Learning Residual Memoryfor Context Compaction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Compactions fall from 56 to 45 alongside
fewer responses (5,336 versus 3,013). Per 1,000 responses, compactions
instead rise from 10.5 to 14.9: the lower total accompanies less interaction.
Mean live context decreases from 105,372 to 101,185 positions including
memory. Fewer calls and smaller contexts explain the lower generation
spend in this record.</p>
</div>
